Profession 21 — Interplanetary Market Designer

A Formal Theory of Asynchronous Trade Under Decoupled Information and Transport Delay

Program: School 7 — Economics of Autonomous Civilizations

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: Gemini, Deepseek, Claude 

Abstract

We develop a formal theory of interplanetary market design that decouples the two physically distinct delay processes governing trade across celestial ports: a fast, light‑speed‑bounded information delay \Delta_{ij}(t) and a slow, propulsion‑bounded transport delay T_{ij}(t) with T_{ij}(t)\gg\Delta_{ij}(t). Four theorems are proved, extending the original three‑theorem structure to include survival constraints, adversarial delay manipulation, a dynamically weighted resource‑basket numeraire, and a price‑responsive coupling to autonomous supply networks.

Theorem 1 (Locality Advantage and Delay‑Discounted Incentive Compatibility).
Any synchronous, fixed‑clock market‑clearing mechanism is structurally non‑neutral with respect to a bidder’s distance from the clearing node. We restore neutrality by embedding bids in a Minkowski‑spacetime discounting framework: each bid’s effective value is v e^{-\lambda\Delta} where \Delta is the light‑travel time from bidder to clearing port. Under truthful reporting of location and a publicly known discount parameter \lambda equal to the expected logarithmic price drift per unit delay, the mechanism is incentive compatible and locality‑neutral.

Theorem 2 (Adaptive HTLC Safety Under Time‑Varying Light Delay).
For an atomic cross‑port swap secured by Hash‑Time‑Locked Contracts (HTLCs), we derive the necessary and sufficient timeout gap when the one‑way information delay \Delta_{ij}(t) is a known, time‑varying function (orbital ephemeris). We prove that setting the refund timeout of the initiating party as T_A = T_B + \max_{t\in[t_{\text{lock}},t_{\text{claim}}]}(\Delta_{ij}(t)+\sigma_i+\sigma_j) guarantees safety against a Byzantine adversary that can delay messages up to the physical light‑speed bound. The result gives an exact, computationally tractable safety condition for interplanetary atomic swaps.

Theorem 3 (No‑Arbitrage Band with Survival‑Premium Caps).
Because information delay is negligible compared with transport delay, price information synchronises rapidly, but price discipline via physical arbitrage is weak. Under a random‑walk price model, the variance of the realised arbitrage profit grows as \sigma_p^2 T_{ij}(t). We extend this to ports with lexicographic survival constraints: if a port’s reserve of a critical resource falls below a survival threshold, its local price becomes infinite. We show that the no‑arbitrage band collapses to a one‑sided bound and prove that an “emergency price cap” mechanism, backed by a system‑wide survival reserve, prevents predatory arbitrage while preserving allocative efficiency.

Theorem 4 (Dynamic Weighted Index Numeraire and ASN Coupling).
A static resource‑basket numeraire is subject to drift as relative resource scarcities evolve. We define a Dynamic Weighted Index (DWI) numeraire V_{\text{num}}(t) = \sum_{k} w_k(t) p_k(t), where the weights w_k(t) are updated continuously to reflect the global marginal survival value of each resource. We prove that forward contracts denominated in DWI minimise the real‑value variance over the transport horizon. Furthermore, the DWI‑denominated forward price serves as a sufficient signal for Autonomous Supply Networks (Profession 20): a price‑responsive routing policy that prioritises cargo along links with the highest forward‑spread per unit delay achieves global allocative efficiency in the closed‑loop system.

The theorems dictate an architecture of three layers: local synchronous auctions, delay‑discounted cross‑port order books, and DWI‑denominated deferred forward settlement with adaptive HTLCs, integrated with price‑responsive supply‑network routing.

