Chapter 1. Introduction: Two Delays, One Economy

Nearly all prior treatments of communication delay in space systems collapse two physically distinct phenomena into one. Information delay is bounded by the speed of light: Earth–Mars communication ranges from roughly 3 to 22 minutes depending on orbital geometry. Transport delay, bounded by propulsion physics, is orders of magnitude larger: cargo transit follows transfer‑window geometry measured in months. The gap between \Delta (information) and T (transport) is the central object of this dissertation.

Existing “interplanetary internet” research treats \Delta as the binding constraint and designs delay‑tolerant networking protocols around it. Space‑logistics research (the MIT Space Logistics node‑and‑arc model) treats T as the binding constraint and optimises physical routing and buffer stock. Neither asks the market‑design question directly: given that \Delta is fast and T is slow, what trading mechanism correctly prices a good whose information is available quickly but whose physical delivery is not?

This dissertation provides the first unified answer. It also incorporates, from the outset, three features that a viable interplanetary economy must possess:

1. Lexicographic survival preferences – ports will not maximise profit at the expense of critical life‑support reserves.
2. Strategic delay manipulation – agents may attempt to game information propagation to create artificial locality advantages.
3. A stable, system‑wide numeraire – a unit of account that remains meaningful across months‑long transport horizons and heterogeneous port endowments.

These features, together with the three‑layer architecture, define the field of Profession 21.

