Chapter 2. Formal Model of an Interplanetary Market System (IMS)

Definition 2.1 (Interplanetary Market System).
An IMS is a tuple

\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{P},\mathcal{K},\Delta,T,\{p_i\},c,\{S_{i,k}\},\mathcal{A})

where

· \mathcal{P} is a finite set of ports (Earth, Lunar, Martian, belt stations);
· \mathcal{K} is a finite set of tradeable resources, partitioned into critical (life‑support) and non‑critical subsets;
· \Delta:\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{P}\times\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} is the one‑way light‑travel time between ports at emission time t (symmetric \Delta_{ij}(t)=\Delta_{ji}(t) in vacuum, but effective \Delta may include network‑layer store‑and‑forward delays; we use the physical floor for safety proofs);
· T:\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{P}\times\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} is the physical transport delay for moving a unit of tradeable goods, governed by orbital transfer‑window geometry and generally asymmetric;
· p_i:\mathcal{K}\times\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} is the local clearing‑price process at port i;
· c:\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{P}\times\mathcal{K}\times\mathbb{R}_{\ge0}\to\mathbb{R}_{\ge0} is the per‑unit transport cost;
· S_{i,k}\in\mathbb{R}_{>0} is the survival threshold for port i of resource k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}};
· \mathcal{A} is the set of agents, each characterised by a lexicographic survival‑constrained utility function.

Standing Assumption A1 (Delay Decoupling).
T_{ij}(t)\ge\kappa\,\Delta_{ij}(t) for a constant \kappa\gg1 (empirically \kappa\sim10^4–10^5 for Earth–Mars). This is used in Theorem 3; Theorems 1 and 2 hold for arbitrary positive \Delta.

Assumption A2 (Public Ephemeris).
The functions \Delta_{ij}(t) and T_{ij}(t) are deterministic, publicly known, and continuously differentiable, derived from orbital mechanics. (In the adversarial extension, an agent may attempt to manipulate the effective delay by adding artificial processing latency, but the physical floor remains common knowledge.)

2.1 Lexicographic Survival Utility

Each agent (or port authority) has preferences over resource holdings that are lexicographic: survival constraints dominate profit.

Definition 2.2 (Lexicographic Preference Order).
For agent a at port i, let x_{i,k} denote her final holding of resource k. Critical resources are indexed k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}. The agent’s preferences are represented by the binary relation \succ such that:

1. If for any k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}, x_{i,k}<S_{i,k}, then any allocation that meets the threshold is strictly preferred, regardless of other resources.
2. Subject to all critical thresholds being satisfied, preferences over non‑critical resources and additional amounts of critical resources follow a standard quasi‑linear utility:

U_i(x,m)=m+\sum_{k}v_{i,k}(x_{i,k})

where m is a numeraire (say, DWI‑denominated credits) and v_{i,k} are increasing, concave valuation functions.

Implication for Demand.
When a port’s reserve of a critical resource falls below S_{i,k}, its willingness‑to‑pay for that resource becomes effectively infinite; the local price rises without bound until the threshold is restored. This creates “survival premium” spikes that market mechanisms must handle without enabling predatory exploitation.

2.2 Strategic Delay and Adversarial Model

We extend the model to allow agents to manipulate effective information delay by introducing artificial settlement/confirmation latency \sigma_i or by delaying message forwarding. While the physical light‑delay \Delta_{ij}(t) is immutable, an agent controlling a communication relay or consensus node may choose to add a processing lag \delta_i^{\text{art}} to its reported timestamps or confirmation times.

Definition 2.3 (Strategic Delay Game).
Each port i is controlled by a strategic operator who chooses an artificial latency parameter \alpha_i\in[0,\bar\alpha] that is added to all outgoing messages and settlement confirmations. The effective one‑way information delay becomes \tilde\Delta_{ij}(t)=\Delta_{ij}(t)+\alpha_i. The operator’s payoff is the profit from trading in the cross‑port market, which may increase with a locality advantage (Theorem 1). The system designer can audit message timestamps using the public ephemeris; misreporting \alpha_i carries a penalty.

We will design mechanisms that are latency‑dominant‑strategy truthful: honest reporting of \alpha_i=0 is a dominant strategy.

