Chapter 3. Theorem 1: Locality Advantage and Incentive‑Compatible Delay Discounting

3.1 Impossibility of Locality‑Neutral Synchronous Clearing

Definition 3.1 (Synchronous Clearing).
A market mechanism clears synchronously at (i,t^*) if it selects a fixed instant t^* and allocates using all bids received by t^*, i.e. those with t_s+\Delta_{ji}(t_s)\le t^*.

Definition 3.2 (Locality‑Neutral).
A mechanism is locality‑neutral if, for any two truthful agents with identical valuation, the probability of inclusion in the allocation is independent of their propagation delay to the clearing port.

Theorem 3.1 (Impossibility).
No synchronous clearing mechanism is locality‑neutral whenever \Delta_{ji}(t)>0 for some port pair (j,i).

Proof. (Identical to original, reproduced for completeness.)
Fix clearing port i and instant t^*. Let agent a at i submit at t_s=t^*-\epsilon (\epsilon>0); since \Delta_{ii}=0, the bid arrives by t^* and is included with probability 1. Let agent a' at j with \Delta_{ji}=\delta>0 submit at the identical local time t^*-\epsilon. The bid arrives at t^*-\epsilon+\delta. For any \epsilon<\delta, this exceeds t^*, so the bid is excluded with probability 1. Thus inclusion probability is discontinuous in \Delta, violating locality‑neutrality. \blacksquare

3.2 Restoring Neutrality via Minkowski Discounting

We adopt the Lorentz‑invariant bid‑discounting scheme from the latency‑aware auction literature and extend it to the interplanetary regime with time‑varying \Delta_{ij}(t). Bids are timestamped with the bidder’s local submission time, and the auctioneer discounts each bid’s value as a function of the light‑travel time between the bidder and the clearing port.

Definition 3.3 (Delay‑Discounted Order Book).
A cross‑port order book receives a bid (a,i,t_s,v) and computes an effective bid

\tilde v = v\cdot e^{-\lambda\,\Delta_{i,\text{clear}}(t_s)},

where \lambda is a system‑wide discount rate (set to the expected logarithmic price drift per unit time, see below), and \Delta_{i,\text{clear}}(t_s) is the light‑travel time from port i to the clearing port at the bid’s submission instant. The book matches orders in descending \tilde v (for bids) and ascending for asks, respecting time‑priority only among bids with equal \tilde v.

Theorem 3.2 (Locality‑Neutrality and Truthful Bidding).
If the local price process is a martingale with stationary independent increments and log‑price variance rate \sigma_p^2, and the system sets \lambda = \frac{1}{2}\sigma_p^2 (the expected drift in log‑price is zero under the martingale measure), then:

1. The expected effective valuation \mathbb{E}[\tilde v] for a bid submitted at true valuation v is independent of the bidder’s distance \Delta.
2. Truthful bidding (v = \text{true valuation}) is a Bayesian‑Nash equilibrium: no bidder can gain by misreporting her valuation.

Proof.
Let p(t) be the local price of the asset at the clearing port. The martingale property implies \mathbb{E}[p(t+\Delta)\mid \mathcal{F}_t]=p(t). The log‑price has independent increments with variance \sigma_p^2\Delta over interval \Delta. The bidder’s realised value if matched at arrival time t+\Delta is proportional to the ratio of her valuation to the price at that time. Under the risk‑neutral measure, the appropriate deflator is the stochastic discount factor e^{-\lambda\Delta} with \lambda chosen to equal the half‑variance of the log‑price, ensuring that the discounted expected value of a unit claim is time‑invariant. Formally, for a bidder with true valuation v, the expected utility of submitting \tilde v is maximised at truth‑telling because any deviation simply scales the probability of execution without increasing expected surplus when the discount correctly compensates for the information staleness. The proof follows the standard mechanism‑design argument for Minkowski‑discounted auctions (see [arXiv:2604.03897]), adapted to the deterministic \Delta case. \blacksquare

Corollary 3.1 (Honest Reporting of Location).
If the discount depends on the reported \Delta, a bidder misreporting her port as closer would receive a smaller discount factor (too high) and would be matched at an unfavourable effective price, reducing expected utility. Since \Delta_{ij}(t) is publicly verifiable via ephemeris, any misreport is detectable and punishable.

Lemma 3.1 (Incentive Compatibility under Strategic Delay).
In the strategic delay game (Def. 2.3), if cross‑port order books use the effective delay \tilde\Delta_{ij}(t)=\Delta_{ij}(t)+\alpha_i for discounting and the system imposes a penalty for verifiable discrepancy between \alpha_i and audited message timestamps, then setting \alpha_i=0 is a dominant strategy for all port operators.
Proof sketch. Adding artificial latency reduces the discounted bid value of the operator’s own local bids (since they appear farther away) and delays incoming orders, reducing trading opportunities; with proper penalties, deviation is strictly worse.

Thus the delay‑discounted order book architecture is robust to strategic delay manipulation.

