Chapter 4. Theorem 2: Adaptive HTLC Safety Under Time‑Varying Light Delay

4.1 Setup and Necessity

Atomic cross‑port swaps use Hash‑Time‑Locked Contracts (HTLCs). Party A at port i locks asset X with hash‑lock h=H(s) and refund timeout T_A. Party B at port j, upon observing the lock, locks asset Y with the same hash‑lock and a shorter timeout T_B<T_A. The secret s is revealed by A to claim Y; B then uses the revealed s to claim X before T_A. Let \sigma_i,\sigma_j be worst‑case settlement finality times at the respective ports (the time after which a transaction is probabilistically irreversible with a target security parameter, e.g. six confirmations on a blockchain).

Proposition 4.1 (Necessity).
If T_A-T_B < \Delta_{ij}(t_{\text{lock}})+\sigma_i+\sigma_j, there exists an honest execution trace in which atomicity fails.

Proof. As in original: A reveals s at T_B-\sigma_j; B’s claim on i can complete no earlier than (T_B-\sigma_j)+\Delta_{ij}+\sigma_i. If this exceeds T_A, A can also reclaim X. \blacksquare

4.2 Sufficiency with Time‑Varying \Delta_{ij}(t)

The terrestrial sufficiency argument assumes a fixed, known upper bound on message delay. In the interplanetary case, \Delta_{ij}(t) is a deterministic function given by ephemeris data. We can therefore compute an exact, time‑varying safety margin.

Definition 4.1 (Adaptive Timeout Gap).
Let t_{\text{lock}} be the time A initiates the HTLC. Let t_{\text{claim}} be the latest time B might need to claim (the moment A reveals s, which can be as late as T_B-\sigma_j). The adaptive gap is

G(t_{\text{lock}},T_B)=\max_{t\in[t_{\text{lock}},\,T_B-\sigma_j]}\bigl(\Delta_{ij}(t)+\sigma_i+\sigma_j\bigr).

Set T_A = T_B + G(t_{\text{lock}},T_B).

Theorem 4.1 (Adaptive HTLC Safety).
Under the adaptive timeout gap defined above, the atomic swap is safe against a Byzantine adversary that (i) controls party A and can choose the secret‑reveal time arbitrarily, (ii) can delay any message up to the physical light‑speed floor but not beyond. Atomicity holds: either both parties receive the counterparty asset or both receive a full refund.

Proof.
Assume A behaves adversarially to break atomicity. To succeed, A must claim Y (at j) and also reclaim X (at i). Claiming Y requires revealing s at some time \tau\le T_B-\sigma_j, such that the claim transaction settles by T_B. Once s is revealed, the earliest it can be observed by B at port i is \tau+\Delta_{ij}(\tau). B then submits a claim on X and it settles by \tau+\Delta_{ij}(\tau)+\sigma_i. For A to reclaim X, the refund must become active before B’s claim settles, i.e. we need \tau+\Delta_{ij}(\tau)+\sigma_i > T_A. Substituting T_A = T_B + G:

\tau+\Delta_{ij}(\tau)+\sigma_i > T_B + \max_{t\in[t_{\text{lock}},T_B-\sigma_j]}(\Delta_{ij}(t)+\sigma_i+\sigma_j).

But \tau lies in the maximisation interval, so the right‑hand side is at least \tau+\Delta_{ij}(\tau)+\sigma_i+\sigma_j, implying 0>\sigma_j, a contradiction. Hence no such \tau exists. If A does not reveal s before T_B, A cannot claim Y; both parties eventually receive refunds. Thus atomicity is guaranteed. \blacksquare

Remark. Because \Delta_{ij}(t) is known in advance from orbital ephemeris, the timeout parameters can be set at lock time. The required gap is typically tens of minutes for Earth–Mars, which is trivial compared with T_{ij} (months) but essential for information‑only asset swaps where transport delay is zero. This theorem eliminates the “open problem” of sufficiency for the interplanetary setting: the deterministic delay function makes sufficiency exact.

Corollary 4.1 (Multi‑hop Swaps).
For a chain of HTLCs across multiple ports with delays \Delta_{12},\Delta_{23},\dots, the timeout of each successive hop must include the cumulative maximum light‑delay along the return path, computed analogously. The construction extends directly.

