Chapter 5. Theorem 3: No‑Arbitrage Band with Survival‑Premium Caps and Dynamic Numeraire

5.1 Classical No‑Arbitrage Band and Transport Widening

Theorem 5.1 (Widening of the No‑Arbitrage Band).
Under Assumption A1, and modelling the local price process p_i(t) as a martingale with stationary independent increments of variance rate \sigma_p^2, the variance of the realised arbitrage profit from shipping at t and selling at t+T_{ij} is \sigma_p^2 T_{ij}(t). Consequently, the no‑arbitrage band — the range of price spreads within which rational risk‑averse arbitrageurs will not trade — widens as \sqrt{T_{ij}}, in contrast to the tight band in terrestrial commodity markets.

Proof.
Decompose p_j(t+T_{ij})-p_i(t)-c_{ij}. The innovation over [t,t+T_{ij}] has variance \sigma_p^2 T_{ij}. A risk‑averse arbitrageur demands a positive expected return proportional to standard deviation, so the threshold spread grows as \sqrt{T_{ij}}. \blacksquare

5.2 Incorporating Lexicographic Survival Premiums

When a port’s stock of a critical resource falls below S_{i,k}, its local price becomes unbounded (infinite willingness to pay). This violates the martingale assumption locally. We model the price process with a reflecting boundary: once the reserve hits zero, price is infinite and the port enters an emergency state.

Definition 5.1 (Survival‑Adjusted Price).
Define the survival indicator I_{i,k}(t)=1 if stock \ge S_{i,k}, else 0. The local effective price is

\tilde p_{i,k}(t)=\begin{cases}
p_{i,k}(t) & \text{if } I_{i,k}(t)=1,\\
\infty & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}

Theorem 5.2 (Survival Caps on Arbitrage).
If port j enters an emergency state for resource k (stock < S_{j,k}), no finite‑price arbitrage shipment from i to j can restore allocative efficiency — the emergency price will dominate. However, the system can implement a global emergency cap: a pre‑committed survival reserve held by a system‑wide institution that can sell from its stock at a predefined maximum price P_{\max}, thereby restoring a bounded price and preventing infinite arbitrage rents. Under such a cap, the no‑arbitrage band around P_{\max} widens as before, and no agent can profit by withholding supplies during a survival crisis.

Proof.
Infinite price implies that any finite shipment yields unbounded profit on paper, but the seller cannot extract infinite surplus because the counterparty has finite assets. The global cap bounds the transaction price, eliminating the predatory incentive. The cap must be backed by a physical reserve large enough to meet all emergency demands; its sizing is a Profession 20 problem. \blacksquare

Corollary 5.1 (Mechanism Design for Survival).
The market architecture must include an emergency‑price‑cap mechanism triggered by survival indicators, coupled with mandatory release from system‑wide reserves, ensuring that no port can hold another hostage.

5.3 Dynamic Weighted Index Numeraire

A static resource‑basket numeraire will drift as relative scarcities change. We propose a Dynamic Weighted Index (DWI) that automatically rebalances to track the marginal survival value of resources across the system.

Definition 5.2 (DWI Numeraire).
Let the set of critical resources be \mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}. For each resource k, define its global scarcity signal as the system‑wide marginal survival value:

w_k(t)=\frac{\partial U_{\text{global}}}{\partial Q_k(t)},

where U_{\text{global}} is an agreed‑upon social welfare function (e.g., a weighted sum of port utilities with the lexicographic survival constraint dominant). In practice, w_k(t) can be approximated by the ratio of the number of ports below their survival threshold plus a smoothed estimate of demand elasticity. The DWI is then

V_{\text{num}}(t)=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}} w_k(t)\,p_k(t),

where p_k(t) is an index of the average price of resource k across major ports, computed from the delay‑discounted order books.

Lemma 5.1 (Stabilisation Property).
Under mild regularity conditions, forward contracts for physical delivery denominated in DWI have the minimal variance of real value among all numeraire baskets that respect survival preferences, because the weights w_k correspond to the Lagrange multipliers of the system‑wide survival constraint.

Proof sketch. The optimal numeraire for hedging real consumption is the ideal consumer price index, which weights goods by marginal utility. In our lexicographic model, the marginal utility of a critical resource is zero above the survival threshold and infinite below; this discontinuity is smoothed by the global scarcity signal. The DWI approximates the Pareto‑optimal price index, minimising the deadweight loss from unexpected inflation over the transport horizon. \blacksquare

5.4 Numeraire and No‑Arbitrage

Because forward contracts are denominated in DWI, the effective no‑arbitrage band for physical cargo becomes a band on real (DWI) spreads, which are stabilised by the auto‑rebalancing weights. This aligns incentives: shippers who bear price risk over T_{ij} are compensated in a unit that preserves their future purchasing power of survival goods.

