Chapter 6. Architectural Synthesis: Four Layers with Price‑Responsive Routing

The formal results dictate the following four‑layer architecture.

1. Local synchronous double auctions – at each port, trading occurs with zero cross‑port latency, for immediate or same‑orbit delivery. Locality‑neutrality is trivial because all participants are co‑located.
2. Delay‑discounted cross‑port order books – bids and asks are propagated between ports with discounting \tilde v = v e^{-\lambda\Delta_{i,\text{clear}}}. The discount parameter \lambda is set from the local price volatility. Honest reporting of location is enforced by ephemeris verification, rendering strategic delay unprofitable.
3. Deferred forward‑settlement layer with adaptive HTLCs – contracts mature at physical arrival t+T_{ij}(t) and are secured by HTLCs whose timeouts satisfy the adaptive gap condition of Theorem 4.1. Settlement is in DWI, insulating parties from spurious currency risk.
4. Price‑responsive supply‑network integration – the DWI‑denominated forward spread F_{ij}(t)= \mathbb{E}_t[V_{\text{num}}(t+T_{ij})\,p_j(t+T_{ij})] - p_i(t) - c_{ij} provides a direct arbitrage signal for the Autonomous Supply Network (Profession 20). We formalise this as:

Definition 6.1 (Price‑Responsive Routing Policy).
An ASN router receives a set of transport links with capacities C_{ij} and transit times T_{ij}. It assigns cargo flows x_{ij}(t) to maximise the instantaneous value of the forward‑spread‑weighted throughput:

\max \sum_{i,j} x_{ij}(t) \cdot \bigl(F_{ij}(t) - \kappa \cdot \text{Var}(F_{ij}(t))\bigr),

subject to capacity and buffer constraints, where \kappa reflects the network operator’s risk aversion.

Theorem 6.1 (Allocative Efficiency).
If the forward market is complete and arbitrageurs quote spreads F_{ij} that satisfy no‑arbitrage, the price‑responsive routing policy combined with the DWI forward settlement achieves globally allocative‑efficient physical flows: goods move from ports with low marginal survival value to ports with high marginal survival value, and no alternative routing can Pareto‑improve the system’s survival‑weighted welfare.
Proof. The Lagrangian of the ASN routing problem with DWI‑denominated values coincides with the welfare maximisation problem of the system planner under lexicographic preferences. The forward spreads equal the shadow prices of the resource constraints; thus the routing policy is decentralised but efficient. \blacksquare

This unified layer closes the loop between market design and autonomous logistics, fulfilling the synthesis of Professions 19, 20, and 21.

