Chapter 7. Open Problems

1. Relativistic Time Synchronisation. The delay‑discounted order book and HTLCs rely on a common time coordinate across ports. In general relativity, defining a global “now” is non‑trivial. Extending the market mechanism to operate with only local proper times and invariant spacetime intervals (as in the Minkowski‑auction literature) is an open formal challenge.
2. Dynamic Weight Update Mechanism. The DWI weights must be updated in real time from decentralised observations of port reserves and emergency states. Designing a tamper‑proof, Byzantine‑resilient oracle system to compute w_k(t) without central control is an open problem in distributed systems and cryptoeconomics.
3. Survival Reserve Sizing. The global emergency cap requires an adequately sized system‑wide reserve. Determining the optimal reserve level under multi‑port, multi‑resource demand shocks, and its interaction with the no‑arbitrage band, is a stochastic control problem that bridges Professions 20 and 21.
4. Empirical Calibration. The martingale model for interplanetary commodity prices lacks empirical foundation until active markets exist. Laboratory experiments and agent‑based simulations can test the robustness of the theorems before deployment.

