Appendix A. Formal Model Extensions

A.1 Probability Space and Price Processes

Let (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\ge0},\mathbb{P}) be a filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions. The local clearing-price process for each resource k at port i is a cadlag \mathcal{F}_t-adapted process p_{i,k}(t,\omega). Unless otherwise specified, we work under the physical measure \mathbb{P}.

Assumption A.3 (Martingale Property).
For each i and k, the discounted price process e^{-rt}p_{i,k}(t) is an \mathcal{F}_t-martingale, where r is the system-wide risk-free rate (often zero for resource markets). Equivalently, the undiscounted price p_{i,k}(t) is a martingale after removing any deterministic drift due to storage costs, which we absorb into the transport cost c_{ij}.

For the proofs of Theorem 1 and 3, we employ a reduced-form model: the log-price \log p_{i,k}(t) has stationary independent increments with variance rate \sigma_p^2. This is consistent with a geometric Brownian motion without drift under the martingale measure.

A.2 Lexicographic Utility Representation

Definition A.1 (Lexicographic Survival Preference).
For agent a at port i, let x = (x_k)_{k\in\mathcal{K}} be a vector of final resource holdings and m the holding of the numeraire (DWI credits). Let \mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}\subseteq\mathcal{K} be the set of critical resources. Define the binary relation \succeq as:

1. Survival dominance: If there exists k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}} such that x_k < S_{i,k} and x'_k \ge S_{i,k}, then x' \succ x regardless of other components.
2. Tie-breaking within survival region: If for all k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}, both x_k \ge S_{i,k} and x'_k \ge S_{i,k}, then preferences reduce to a continuous utility function U_i(x,m) = m + \sum_k v_{i,k}(x_k), where each v_{i,k} is strictly increasing, concave, and continuously differentiable on [S_{i,k},\infty).

This lexicographic order is not representable by a single real-valued utility function, but it can be handled via constrained maximization: an agent first ensures x_k \ge S_{i,k} for all k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}, and subject to that, maximizes the continuous utility component.

