Appendix C. Proofs for Theorem 2: Adaptive HTLC Safety

C.1 Formal HTLC Model

Hash-Time-Locked Contract (HTLC) on port i:

· Lock: A transaction TX_{\text{lock}}^i from party to a script that can be redeemed by either:
  1. providing a preimage s such that H(s)=h and a signature from the counterparty, OR
  2. after time T_{\text{refund}} (absolute time), a signature from the original owner (refund path).
· Claim: To claim the locked asset, a party broadcasts a transaction with the preimage s. This transaction must be confirmed (achieve finality) by the local consensus mechanism.
· Settlement finality time \sigma_i: We assume a worst-case upper bound on the time from broadcast to irreversible inclusion in the ledger, given the security parameter. In proof-of-work chains, this is the time for k confirmations; in BFT chains, it's the consensus round time.

Atomic Swap between A (port i) and B (port j):

1. A generates random secret s, computes h = H(s).
2. A deploys HTLC_X on port i locking X with hashlock h, refund timeout T_A.
3. B observes HTLC_X on port i (after propagation delay \Delta_{ij}(t_1)) and verifies correctness.
4. B deploys HTLC_Y on port j locking Y with same hashlock h, refund timeout T_B < T_A.
5. A observes HTLC_Y on port j (delay \Delta_{ji}(t_2)).
6. A claims Y by revealing s on port j before T_B. B sees s and claims X on port i before T_A.
7. If A does not claim Y by T_B, both parties can refund after their respective timeouts.

C.2 Necessity Proof (Proposition 4.1)

Restatement: If T_A - T_B < \Delta_{ij}(t_{\text{lock}}) + \sigma_i + \sigma_j, there exists an honest execution where atomicity fails.

Detailed proof.
Assume A acts honestly up to the point of claiming Y at the last safe moment. That is, A broadcasts the claim transaction for Y on port j at local time \tau = T_B - \sigma_j - \epsilon (with infinitesimal \epsilon>0 to ensure settlement by T_B). The claim is included in the ledger by T_B. Immediately upon confirmation, the secret s is visible on port j. B must now claim X on port i. The earliest B can observe s is \tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) (the light-travel time). B then constructs and broadcasts the claim transaction on port i; this takes negligible processing time. The claim will settle on port i no earlier than \tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) + \sigma_i. For the swap to be atomic, B's claim must settle before A's refund timeout T_A expires. If T_A is such that

\tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) + \sigma_i > T_A,

then B's claim cannot settle in time. Since \tau can be arbitrarily close to T_B - \sigma_j, the condition for failure is:

(T_B - \sigma_j) + \min_{\tau\text{ near }T_B-\sigma_j}\Delta_{ij}(\tau) + \sigma_i > T_A.

Taking \Delta_{ij} as continuous, this becomes T_B - \sigma_j + \Delta_{ij}(T_B-\sigma_j) + \sigma_i > T_A. If T_A - T_B < \Delta_{ij}(T_B-\sigma_j) + \sigma_i + \sigma_j, the inequality holds, and atomicity is broken: A gets Y (claimed) and also can later refund X after T_A, while B loses Y and gets nothing. \blacksquare

C.3 Sufficiency with Adaptive Gap (Theorem 4.1)

Adversary model. The adversary controls party A and can choose any secret-reveal time \tau as long as it follows protocol (i.e., \tau \le T_B - \sigma_j if A wishes to claim Y). The adversary can delay any message by adding up to the physical minimum latency; i.e., a message sent at time t from i to j cannot arrive before t+\Delta_{ij}(t). The adversary cannot make messages travel faster than light. This is the standard Byzantine adversary with bounded delay.

Theorem (Restated). Define T_A = T_B + \max_{t\in[t_{\text{lock}}, T_B - \sigma_j]} (\Delta_{ij}(t) + \sigma_i + \sigma_j). Then the atomic swap is safe.

Proof.
We must show that if A claims Y, then B can always claim X before T_A; and if A does not claim Y by T_B, then both can refund.

· Case 1: A reveals s at time \tau \le T_B - \sigma_j to claim Y.
    The claim transaction on port j settles by \tau + \sigma_j \le T_B. So A successfully claims Y. The secret s becomes known on port j at time \tau (as soon as the transaction is broadcast). The earliest B can learn s at port i is \tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) due to light-speed propagation. B then immediately broadcasts the claim on port i; the earliest settlement time on i is \tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) + \sigma_i. We need this to be \le T_A. By definition,
  \tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) + \sigma_i \le T_B + \max_{t\in[t_{\text{lock}}, T_B-\sigma_j]} (\Delta_{ij}(t) + \sigma_i + \sigma_j).
  Since \tau is within the maximization interval, the RHS is at least \tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) + \sigma_i + \sigma_j \ge \tau + \Delta_{ij}(\tau) + \sigma_i. So the settlement occurs before T_A. Therefore B successfully claims X.
· Case 2: A does not reveal s by T_B - \sigma_j (or at all).
    Then A cannot claim Y because the timeout T_B will pass without a valid claim. After T_B, B can refund Y using its refund path. After T_A, A can refund X. Atomicity holds (both get refunds).

Thus in all cases, either both get the counterparty asset or both get refunds, satisfying atomicity. \blacksquare

C.4 Multi-Hop Extension

Consider a chain of swaps: P_0 \leftrightarrow P_1 \leftrightarrow \dots \leftrightarrow P_n. For each hop k\to k+1, we set timeouts with decreasing values along the chain. The initiator P_0 sets a timeout T_0; each subsequent party sets a timeout T_{k+1} strictly less than T_k, with gaps that account for the cumulative maximum light-delay for the return path of the secret.

Corollary C.1 (Multi-Hop Safety).
Define T_{k} = T_{k+1} + \max_{t\in I_{k+1}} (\Delta_{k+1,k}(t) + \sigma_{k+1} + \sigma_k) where the interval is from lock time of hop k+1 to its claim deadline. Then the entire chain is atomic.
Proof. By backward induction, applying Theorem 4.1 to each adjacent pair. \blacksquare

