Appendix D. Proofs for Theorem 3: No-Arbitrage Band, Survival Premiums, and Dynamic Numeraire

D.1 Variance of Arbitrage Profit (Theorem 5.1)

Model. Consider a single resource. The price at port i is p_i(t). Under the martingale assumption (A.3), p_i(t) is a driftless Itô process: dp_i(t) = \sigma_p p_i(t) dW(t). (Geometric Brownian motion.) The price at port j is linked to p_i via the arbitrage relationship, but we assume that due to the wide band, they can diverge.

An arbitrageur observes at time t a spread S(t) = p_j(t) - p_i(t) - c_{ij} > 0. She buys at i for p_i(t), pays transport cost c_{ij}, and ships; the goods arrive at j at time t+T_{ij}. She sells at the prevailing spot price p_j(t+T_{ij}). Her profit (undiscounted) is

\Pi = p_j(t+T_{ij}) - p_i(t) - c_{ij}.

Since T_{ij} is large, she cannot hedge this risk perfectly. Under the physical measure \mathbb{P}, the expected profit is \mathbb{E}_t[\Pi] = \mathbb{E}_t[p_j(t+T_{ij})] - p_i(t) - c_{ij}. If the market is efficient, risk-neutral arbitrageurs would ensure that no expected profit exists, i.e., \mathbb{E}_t[p_j(t+T_{ij})] = p_i(t) + c_{ij}. But risk-averse arbitrageurs demand a premium.

The variance of \Pi is \text{Var}_t(p_j(t+T_{ij})). If p_j follows a martingale with independent increments, its variance over an interval of length T_{ij} is proportional to T_{ij}. Specifically, with the geometric Brownian motion, the variance of the price level is p_j(t)^2(e^{\sigma_p^2 T_{ij}} - 1) \approx p_j^2 \sigma_p^2 T_{ij} for small \sigma_p^2 T_{ij}. The standard deviation scales with \sqrt{T_{ij}}. A risk-averse arbitrageur with CARA utility and risk aversion \gamma will only enter if the expected profit exceeds \frac{\gamma}{2}\text{Var}(\Pi). Thus the no-arbitrage band half-width is at least \frac{\gamma}{2}\sigma_p^2 p_j^2 T_{ij} in variance terms, or \gamma\sigma_p^2 p_j^2 T_{ij}/2. This grows linearly with T_{ij} in variance, implying the band in price terms widens as \sqrt{T_{ij}}. This proves Theorem 5.1.

D.2 Survival Premium and Emergency Price Cap (Theorem 5.2)

Definition D.1 (Emergency State).
Port j enters an emergency state for resource k at time t if its current stock Q_{j,k}(t) drops below the survival threshold S_{j,k}. In this state, the local price p_{j,k}(t) becomes effectively infinite because the port’s willingness to pay is unbounded (lexicographic preference). We model the market as exhibiting a vertical demand curve at the minimal survival quantity.

Lemma D.1. If port j is in emergency and another port i has surplus, the competitive equilibrium price at j would be infinity, leading to market breakdown. However, no finite arbitrage shipment can capture infinite profit because the counterparty has finite assets; the actual transaction would be limited by j's total wealth.

To prevent exploitative pricing and ensure survival, we introduce a Global Emergency Reserve (GER) holding a stock Q^{\text{ger}}_k of resource k. The system‑wide protocol mandates that if any port enters emergency, the GER will sell the resource to that port at a predetermined emergency price cap P_{\max,k}, which is set to a level that is high enough to incentivize ordinary shipments but not infinite.

Proof of Theorem 5.2 (Survival Caps on Arbitrage).
Suppose port j is in emergency and an arbitrageur at port i considers shipping. Without cap, the arbitrageur could demand a price equal to j's entire liquid wealth, which might be large but finite. However, the presence of the GER with a cap P_{\max} constrains the maximum price to P_{\max}. The arbitrageur then cannot extract more than P_{\max} - p_i - c_{ij} per unit. The GER effectively acts as a market maker of last resort. The cap also assures that the survival threshold is met, since the port can purchase from the GER. The cap eliminates the “infinite” arbitrage rent. The standard no-arbitrage band around P_{\max} then applies. This proves the theorem. \blacksquare

D.3 Dynamic Weighted Index (DWI) Numeraire

Definition D.2 (Global Welfare Function).
Let the set of ports be \mathcal{P}. The system planner’s objective is to maximize a lexicographic social welfare function: first ensure for all ports i and all critical resources k, Q_{i,k} \ge S_{i,k}; then maximize \sum_i \sum_k v_{i,k}(Q_{i,k}). The planner can redistribute resources with zero transport cost for the purpose of defining the ideal numeraire (not physically). The marginal social value of resource k at time t is the Lagrange multiplier \mu_k(t) on the global resource constraint \sum_i Q_{i,k} \le \bar Q_k(t) (total system stock). When no emergency constraints bind, \mu_k(t) is the derivative of the sum of concave valuations; if an emergency binds for any port, the multiplier becomes large, reflecting the infinite shadow price.

Definition D.3 (Dynamic Weights).
Define the weight for resource k in the numeraire as w_k(t) = \mu_k(t) / \sum_{\ell} \mu_\ell(t) (normalized). The DWI is then

V_{\text{num}}(t) = \sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}}} w_k(t) \, p_k^{\text{avg}}(t),

where p_k^{\text{avg}}(t) is the volume-weighted average price of resource k across all ports, obtained from the cross-port order books.

Lemma D.2 (Minimal Real-Value Variance).
Consider a forward contract for a fixed bundle of resources delivered at future time t+T. If the contract’s payoff is fixed in DWI units, the variance of its real value (in terms of the future ideal DWI basket) is zero, i.e., it is perfectly hedged against relative price changes.
Proof. The payoff in DWI is by definition the value of the ideal basket. Therefore, a DWI-denominated claim has a real value that stays exactly 1 future DWI, regardless of price fluctuations. So its purchasing power over the survival-relevant resource basket is stable. In contrast, any static numeraire would have a real value that fluctuates with the relative prices. This establishes DWI as the minimum-variance numeraire for long-term contracts. \blacksquare

Connection to forward spreads. In the no-arbitrage condition, the relevant arbitrage spread should be expressed in DWI to correctly reflect the risk borne by shippers: they care about the future purchasing power of their profits. Thus the forward price for delivery at j at time t+T_{ij} in DWI terms equals the expected future DWI price, and the forward spread F_{ij}(t) is defined in DWI. This stabilizes the arbitrage signal.

