Appendix E. Proofs for Theorem 6.1: Price-Responsive Routing and Allocative Efficiency

E.1 ASN Model

The Autonomous Supply Network (ASN) from Profession 20 controls physical flow x_{ij}(t) of each resource along directed links (i,j) with capacity C_{ij}(t) and transit time T_{ij}(t). The ASN also manages buffer stocks at each node. For simplicity, we consider a fluid model in continuous time.

E.2 Welfare Maximization

The system-wide dynamic welfare maximization problem over horizon [0,\tau] is:

\max_{x_{ij}(t),\, b_i(t)} \int_0^\tau \sum_i \sum_k U_{i,k}(b_{i,k}(t)) \, dt

subject to:

\dot Q_{i,k}(t) = \sum_j x_{ji,k}(t-T_{ji}) - \sum_j x_{ij,k}(t) + \text{local production} - b_{i,k}(t),

Q_{i,k}(t) \ge S_{i,k} \quad \text{for } k\in\mathcal{K}_{\text{crit}},

0 \le x_{ij,k}(t) \le C_{ij}(t).

Here b_{i,k}(t) is consumption, providing utility U_{i,k} (which includes lexicographic survival through infinite disutility if stock falls below threshold, captured by a barrier function). For mathematical convenience, we replace the lexicographic constraint with a smooth barrier function that tends to infinity as Q_{i,k} \downarrow S_{i,k}.

The Lagrangian for this problem yields co-state variables (shadow prices) \psi_{i,k}(t) for the stock dynamics. The optimal consumption satisfies U_{i,k}'(b_{i,k}(t)) = \psi_{i,k}(t). The optimal routing condition is: for each link (i,j), flow x_{ij,k}(t) > 0 only if

\psi_{j,k}(t+T_{ij}) - \psi_{i,k}(t) - c_{ij}(t) \ge 0,

with equality if capacity is not fully utilized. These \psi_{i,k}(t) are exactly the marginal social values of resources at each location and time.

E.3 Market Implementation

Now consider the market mechanism from Profession 21. The local competitive price p_{i,k}(t) will, under perfect competition and complete markets, equal the marginal utility of consumption (since consumers optimize). Furthermore, the forward price quoted for delivery at j at time t+T_{ij} should satisfy

F_{ij,k}(t) = \mathbb{E}_t[\psi_{j,k}(t+T_{ij})],

where expectation is under a suitable pricing measure. With complete markets and no arbitrage, the forward price coincides with the expected shadow price.

The forward spread F_{ij,k}(t) - p_{i,k}(t) - c_{ij}(t) in DWI terms (after appropriate conversion using the DWI basket weights) then mirrors the condition \psi_{j,k}(t+T_{ij}) - \psi_{i,k}(t) - c_{ij} \ge 0. Therefore, the price-responsive routing policy that maximizes the risk-adjusted forward spread across links replicates the optimality conditions of the social planner. Formally:

Theorem E.1 (Allocative Efficiency).
Under the assumptions of complete forward markets, no-arbitrage, and DWI-numeraire, the price-responsive routing policy defined by maximizing \sum_{i,j,k} x_{ij,k}(F_{ij,k} - \kappa\text{Var}(F_{ij,k})) subject to capacity yields the same flow pattern as the welfare-optimal solution. Thus the market mechanism achieves global allocative efficiency.

Proof.
At the competitive equilibrium, the local spot prices p_{i,k}(t) equal the shadow values \psi_{i,k}(t). Because the DWI-numeraire is a linear combination of shadow values of critical resources with weights equal to the normalized global marginal values, the forward price in DWI units aligns incentives: the objective function of a profit-maximizing shipper exactly corresponds to the social welfare gradient. The risk-aversion parameter \kappa can be set to the planner's risk aversion; if shippers are risk-neutral and forward markets are unbiased, then \kappa=0 and the policy maximizes the expected net social gain. This completes the proof. \blacksquare

