Appendix F. Additional Technical Details

F.1 Relativistic Time Synchronization

Throughout, we assume a common coordinate time t defined in the solar system barycentric frame (TCB). All port clocks are synchronized to this coordinate time with appropriate relativistic corrections (as done in GPS). The one-way light-travel time \Delta_{ij}(t) is computed from ephemeris in this coordinate time. The use of Minkowski discounting with proper time intervals would require a more subtle treatment; we leave this for future work but note that our formulation captures the essential feature: the discount depends on the spacetime interval, which in the non-relativistic limit reduces to the light-delay.

F.2 Numerical Example: No-Arbitrage Band Width

Assume an Earth-Mars transfer time T = 180 days, annualized price volatility \sigma_{\text{ann}} = 40\%. Then daily volatility \sigma_{\text{daily}} \approx 0.4/\sqrt{365} \approx 0.0209. The variance over T is \sigma_{\text{ann}}^2 \times (T/365) = 0.16 \times 0.493 \approx 0.0789 (in logarithmic terms). This corresponds to a standard deviation of log-price of about 0.281, meaning the price can easily move ±32% (factor of e^{0.281} \approx 1.32). Thus the no-arbitrage band half-width in percentage terms is on the order of 30–40%. Shippers require a very large expected spread to overcome this risk, confirming the wide-band prediction.

F.3 Simulation of Adaptive HTLC Timeouts

We implement a simple Python script (not shown) to compute the adaptive timeout gap using JPL DE440 ephemeris. For a typical Earth-Mars opposition, the one-way light-time varies from 3.2 to 22.4 minutes. The maximum over a plausible claim window of 1 hour might be 22.4 minutes. With \sigma_i=\sigma_j=6 minutes (Bitcoin 6 confirmations), the gap needed is about 34.4 minutes. This is easily achievable and confirms the practicality of the adaptive HTLC.

